# adbd as root from the start, on builds made with WITH_ADB_INSECURE and on no other.
#
# `adb root` is not enough while a port is being brought up. On LineageOS adbd asks the
# adbroot_service binder service whether root is allowed, the answer is a switch in the
# developer options, and neither exists on a system that stops before zygote: adbd
# waits for the service and the connection drops. This tablet has a locked bootloader
# and no fastboot flash, so a root shell is the only way to repair anything, and it has
# to be there exactly when the system does not start.
#
# service.adb.root=1 is what `adb root` itself sets; adbd reads it when it starts and,
# on a debuggable build, keeps its privileges (should_drop_privileges). ro.adb.secure
# is 0 only with WITH_ADB_INSECURE, which a release is never built with.
#
# Installed in /system/etc/init, not /vendor/etc/init: there the commands run as
# vendor_init, which may not set this property.
on init && property:ro.adb.secure=0
    setprop service.adb.root 1
